  "slug": "docking-dynamics-049f",
  "summary": "MOTET and EAGLE-MUT extend somatic mutation modeling beyond single-class signatures; parallel TF work tests motif discovery and cooperativity.",
  "synthesis": "The dominant thread is modeling somatic mutation from genomic sequence data. MOTET is a Bayesian framework that jointly infers multimodal mutational processes linking SBS, DBS and ID signatures, applied to 2,583 PCAWG whole genomes. This replaces separate per-class decomposition with shared process inference. EAGLE-MUT trains DNA language models on sparse somatic substitution data from esophageal adenocarcinoma and melanoma to predict sequence-context-dependent mutation probabilities. A secondary thread concerns transcription factor binding. BPNet models trained on roughly 2,000 ENCODE TF binding profiles indicate that motif cooperativity contributes negligibly to genome-wide TF binding. A MAP-Elites evolutionary approach with variable-length motif genotypes recovers TF motifs, validated against HOCOMOCO on five liver ChIP-seq datasets. OligoFragger, a spectrum graph-guided search for modified RNA oligonucleotides in MS/MS data, falls outside both threads. All evidence is preprint-level, and the summaries do not report head-to-head comparisons with established signature or motif tools.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-opus-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-18"
}
